---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SHORTCUTS } from "../game/strings.ts";
import "../themes/tokens.css";
import "../themes/warm.css";
import "../themes/minimal.css";
import "../themes/dark.css";
import "../styles/page.css";

/**
 * The rules, and how this particular table works.
 *
 * Static, no island. It is written for somebody who has never played Klondike
 * as well as for somebody who has played it for forty years and wants to know
 * what the middle button does — the second reader is much the more likely, so
 * the rules are short and the part about *this* game is long.
 *
 * The shortcut table is the one in `src/game/strings.ts`, the same list the
 * `?` overlay renders and `test/game/keyboard.test.ts` presses every key in.
 * A page promising a key the game does not honour is worse than no page.
 */
---

<Layout
  title="How to play — Solitaire"
  description="The rules of Klondike solitaire, and how this one works."
>
  <main class="page">
    <p class="page-back"><a href="/">← Back to the game</a></p>

    <h1>How to play</h1>

    <p>
      Klondike. Get all fifty-two cards onto the four foundations, each one
      built up from its ace to its king in a single suit. That is the whole of
      winning.
    </p>

    <h2>The table</h2>

    <p>
      Seven <strong>columns</strong> across the bottom, dealt one card deeper each:
      one card in the first, seven in the last, and only the bottom card of each face
      up. Above them, the <strong>stock</strong> on the left with the rest of the
      deck face down, the <strong>waste</strong> beside it where drawn cards land,
      and the four <strong>foundations</strong> on the right, one per suit.
    </p>

    <h2>The moves</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Onto a column</strong>: a card goes on one of the next rank up
        and the opposite colour. A red six goes on a black seven. A run already
        built that way moves as a block.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Onto a foundation</strong>: an ace starts it, and after that the
        next rank of the same suit, one card at a time. A card can come back off
        a foundation if a column needs it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Into an empty column</strong>: kings only, and a king already
        sitting alone in one has nowhere useful to go.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turning a card</strong>: uncovering a face-down card in a column
        turns it over, always, immediately. It is part of the move rather than a
        decision.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The stock</strong>: tap it to draw. When it runs out, tap the
        empty space to turn the waste back over and go round again — as often as
        you like.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Playing it here</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Tap a card</strong> and it goes where it most obviously wants to go:
        home to its foundation if it will go, otherwise to the best column that will
        have it. Tap a card that is already home and it comes back down. This is the
        quickest way to play and the one to learn first — on a card this size, tapping
        beats dragging.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drag</strong> if you would rather choose the destination yourself.
        The card follows your finger exactly, and springs back if you let go somewhere
        it cannot land.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Press and hold a column</strong> to fan it right out and read the
        cards buried in it. Let go and it closes; nothing has moved.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Undo</strong> as far back as you like, as many times as you like.
        There is no penalty and no score, so nothing is at stake in trying something.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hint</strong> rings one card worth moving and the space it can move
        into. If there is no move at all it says so — that is a fact about the position,
        not a loss. Nothing here ever tells you that you have lost: you decide when
        a deal is over.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Finish</strong> replaces Hint once every card is face up and the stock
        is spent. At that point the deal is already won, and this plays it out rather
        than making you tap fifty times.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Draw one, or draw three</h2>

    <p>
      Draw one turns a single card at a time and most deals can be won. Draw
      three turns three and you may only play the top of them, which is the
      harder, more traditional game. Both are in the menu, and changing it
      starts a new deal, because the same cards make a genuinely different game
      under each rule.
    </p>

    <h2>Deal numbers</h2>

    <p>
      Every deal has a number, and that number means the same deal on every
      device, forever. Share one from the panel at the end of a game, or put it
      in the address as <code>?deal=24</code>. Replaying a deal you have won
      races you against your own best time.
    </p>

    <p>
      By default deals come from a pool that has been solved in advance, so the
      one you are given can be won. That is a setting, and turning it off deals
      from the whole deck of possibilities — which is the honest version of the
      game, and sometimes an unwinnable one.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is also a <strong>daily deal</strong>: the same one for everybody,
      changing at midnight, with a streak if you keep coming back.
    </p>

    <h2>With a keyboard</h2>

    <p>
      The whole game plays from the keyboard, with no pointer at all. The arrow
      keys move between the thirteen piles — the board is one thing to move
      around in, not fifty-two things to tab through — and space picks up and
      puts down.
    </p>

    <dl class="shortcuts">
      {
        SHORTCUTS.map(({ keys, what }) => (
          <div class="shortcut">
            <dt>
              {keys.map((key) => (
                <kbd>{key}</kbd>
              ))}
            </dt>
            <dd>{what}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p>Pressing <kbd>?</kbd> in the game shows this list without leaving it.</p>

    <h2>Reading the cards</h2>

    <p>
      If the cards are hard to read, the menu has three things worth trying
      before anything else: the <strong>High-contrast</strong> deck, with much larger
      indices; the <strong>Four-colour</strong> deck, which gives every suit its own
      colour so red and black never have to be told apart; and the three tables, one
      of which will suit your screen and your light better than the others.
    </p>

    <p>
      The game is also usable with a screen reader. Each pile describes itself
      in full — how many cards it holds, how many are face down, and what is on
      top — and every move is announced as it happens.
    </p>

    <p class="page-back"><a href="/credits/">Credits and licences</a></p>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page ul {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    padding-left: 22px;
  }

  .page li {
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .shortcuts {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  .shortcut {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 12px;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 6px 0;
  }

  .shortcut dt {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .shortcut dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  kbd {
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1;
    padding: 5px 7px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--chrome-fg) 8%, transparent);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--slot-stroke);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }
</style>
